## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to leverage Vertex AI Feature Store for end-to-end data governance while implementing practical rollback mechanisms in a beginner-friendly context. It evaluates understanding of how to wire together storage, training, deployment, and monitoring with clear guardrails.

## Key Concepts
- Vertex AI Feature Store for centralized feature management
- Dataflow for batch feature updates and governance
- BigQuery for drift monitoring and lineage tracking
- Canary deployments with automated rollback
- Data Catalog for metadata and provenance

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal illustrative snippet (pseudo-code)
import {FeatureStoreServiceClient} from '@google-cloud/aiplatform';

const client = new FeatureStoreServiceClient({project, location});

async function getFeatureValues(entityId) {
  const [entity] = await client.readFeatureValues({
    featurestore: FEATURESTORE_NAME,
    entityType: 'user',
    entityId: entityId,
  });
  return entity.featureValues;
}

async function checkDriftAndRollback(currentModelId, previousModelId) {
  const driftScore = await queryBigQueryDriftMetrics();
  if (driftScore > DRIFT_THRESHOLD) {
    await switchEndpointTraffic(previousModelId);
    console.log('Rolled back due to drift:', driftScore);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical multi-plant deployment, data/model versioning, drift monitoring, canary strategy, and cost governance on GCP.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant data architecture and versioning strategies
- Vertex AI custom training pipelines and endpoint management
- Pub/Sub, Dataflow, BigQuery integration patterns
- Drift detection algorithms and automated rollback mechanisms
- Per-tenant resource quotas and cost-aware autoscaling

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: update endpoint traffic split for canary deployment
const aiplatform = require('@google-cloud/aiplatform');
const client = new aiplatform.v1.EndpointServiceClient();

async function updateTrafficSplit(endpointPath, newModelId, trafficPercentage) {
  const [endpoint] = await client.getEndpoint({ name: endpointPath });

  const deployedModels = endpoint.deployedModels.map(model => ({
    id: model.id,
    ...model
  }));

  const trafficSplit = {
    '0': (100 - trafficPercentage) / 100, // existing model
    '1': trafficPercentage / 100          // new canary model
  };

  await client.deployModel({
    endpoint: endpointPath,
    deployedModel: {
      model: `projects/${project}/locations/${location}/models/${newModelId}`,
      displayName: `plant-a-canary-${Date.now()}`,
      trafficSplit
    }
  });
}
```

## Implementation Considerations
- Data versioning: Implement BigQuery table partitioning and Dataflow snapshot windows
- Model registry: Use Vertex AI Model Registry with semantic versioning per plant
- Drift monitoring: Set up custom metrics and Cloud Monitoring alerts
- Rollback automation: Cloud Functions triggered by drift thresholds
- Cost controls: Per-project budgets and Vertex AI endpoint autoscaling policies

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses the ability to design a privacy-conscious, scalable fraud-detection pipeline across multiple tenants with governance and cost controls.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation
- Privacy-preserving aggregation (secure sum, differential privacy)
- Vertex AI Pipelines and canary rollout
- Dataflow enrichment, BigQuery provenance
- Data Catalog policy tagging
- Drift detection and explainability

## Code Example
